A||||7||burdell1||High Idle
Z||000000||burdell1||04-07-99||10:51 PM||||I have an '85 Fiero GT that idles very high. I read somewhere the you can adjust the idle by moving the screwing a screw in the throttle body, but I am not sure where this screw is at (or if it is safe for the car.)  Any help would be great! <br>
Z||000001||Patrick||04-07-99||11:35 PM||||It may not be something as simple as turning a screw to slow down your idle speed. There is also the IAC (idle air control) valve which is controlled by the ECM. I'm not a "trained" mechanic, so maybe you should see if someone else here has any advice.<p>On my '87 with the 4 banger, there was a cap over the idle adjustment screw which I popped out so that I could adjust this screw. But before you start "screwing" around, you better find out first what is causing your Fiero to idle so high.<p>By the way, how high is high? What is the RPM? Is it always high, or just when it first starts? Does it make a difference whether the engine is hot or cold? Does it surge up and down? These are questions some of the other fellas here may need answered before they can help you.<p>-Patrick<p>
Z||000002||theogre||04-08-99||02:49 PM||||with the exception of a TBI rebuild, or some similar type work, I can think of no reason you should ever have to change setting of the idle stop screw. <p>which motor is this? GT's are usually v6, but  it halps to be sure.
Z||000003||burdell1||04-09-99||11:52 AM||||My RPMs after I first start my car is around 2500-3000 rpms.  If i just let it idle down the road it is around 1500-2000 rpms. And yes, it is a V6<p>
Z||000004||theogre||04-09-99||12:22 PM||||have you done all the obvious.... checked for vac leaks, checked the egr, etc?<p>The car is high, but it's behaving as normal. I.E. a high idle at starting, then settling down. It's just higher than spec. a vac leak can cause that, and assorted other problems, depending on just where it is.<br>
Z||000005||burdell1||04-11-99||03:49 PM||||How do you check for vacuum leaks?  It is hard to listen for it because my car is SO loud.  Also where would it be at?
Z||000006||theogre||04-11-99||10:23 PM||||You will almost never hear a vac leak. they just don't tend to work like that.<p>Some of it's a process of elimination. like plugin off both lines to the vapor can then the EGR, etc. do it one item at a time. Don't forget the break boster lines all the way to the booster.<p>Some of it's simple visual/physical inspection. Take the line off, if it is easy to remove, it's probly week, replace it.<p>remove, and inspect all the hard lines. bend them a bit and see if they crack. if they do they are probly cracked else where as well.<p>Hard lines(semi ridged plastic), soft lines(rubber), and hard line conectors(molded rubber) are all posible problems. All parts are available at local parts stores.<p>Replaceing hard lines with soft lines is permited as long as the soft lines have enough room to bend without kinking.<p>As for testing the EGR, I've never really found a reliable way. I usually just replace it. On cars that are 10-20 years old the EGR isn't long for the world anyway, even if it's not the problem. This makes sure it doesn't become one.<p>Note: replacement EGR valves usualy fit more than one vehical. make sure you follow the installation instructions EXACTLY! Replacement EGR's usually come with a set of "washers" that meter the EGR flow. Using the wrong washer WILL screw up your performance.
Z||000007||Carrolles||04-21-99||12:40 PM||||I had the same problem with my 85se 2m6.  The EGR tube was cracked causing unmetered air to enter the intake manifold.  The ECM would compensate by adding fuel to keep emmissions in check.<br>The tube connects to the underside of the intake manifold on one end and to the EGR valve on the other.  It is usually covered with an insulating blanket that looks like asbestos.  With the engine running spray some WD-40 between the tube and the insulating jacket.  If the tube is leaking it will suck the WD-40 in and the engine will race.<br>The original tube is corrugated stainless steel and isn't very flexible.  It eventually cracks.  It can be welded or simply replaced.  Replacement tubes from FOCOA run around $70.00  I got mine from the Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory (I believe it was an IRM unit) before FOCOA started carrying them and I paid $90.00.  This is a very high quality flexible stainless steel braided hose with stainless steel flanges.<p>I've also read somewhere (possibly on Sketch's Fiero Online Service Guide) that a Camaro tube can be slightly modified to fit.<br>It isn't hard to change but it takes a little patience unbolting the end from the underside  of the intake manifold.<p>Good luck<br>Carroll
